GABPBX Changelog
================

This file lists the changes in each GABPBX release. GABPBX is a fork of
Asterisk whose flagship is chan_sofia, a Sofia-SIP based SIP channel driver
that is a drop-in replacement for chan_sip (it registers as channel
technology SIP). Releases are listed newest first.


## 1.4.1 — WebRTC fork-winner media fix, native peer-to-peer text messaging

This release fixes a WebRTC call-forking media failure and adds native SIP text
messaging between registered users.

**WebRTC fork-winner audio/video fix.** When a call forks to a called extension that has
both a WebRTC (WSS) contact and a plain-UDP contact and the WebRTC contact wins the fork,
the call connected but had no audio or video — the winner leg's DTLS never completed. The
winning WebRTC leg's media socket is stolen onto the surviving channel, but for a bundled
winner (video riding the audio transport, with no separate video RTP) the channel's stale
video file descriptors were not cleared; those closed descriptors are always reported
"ready" by the poller, so the channel's classic last-ready-wins poll kept selecting them
over the live audio/STUN socket, which was therefore never read — ICE never latched and
DTLS never started. A new helper now keeps the channel's media fds a strict derived cache
of the live audio/video RTP instances (setting the live descriptors and clearing absent
ones), re-asserted after the fork steal and after a channel masquerade, so the winner
leg's audio is serviced immediately. Physical↔physical, physical↔WebRTC and
WebRTC↔WebRTC calls all carry two-way audio and video.

**Native peer-to-peer SIP MESSAGE.** An out-of-dialog SIP MESSAGE (text) between two
registered users was received but never delivered (it was answered 405). GABPBX now
natively relays it (RFC 3428): an authenticated sender's MESSAGE whose destination
resolves — through the sender's own subscribe-context hint namespace (the auto-created
BLF/regexten extension→device hints) — to a registered local peer is re-originated to
that peer's live contact(s), all of them, answering 202 Accepted (or 480 when the
destination is registered but currently offline). It still falls through to the opt-in
`message_context` dialplan for anything that is not a local hinted peer. The sender is
authenticated by its digest credentials and screened by any per-peer host ACL; the
relayed message carries the sender's and recipient's short logical extension identities
and the recipient's per-contact routing (including its RFC 3327 Path). Enabled by default
(`message_autorelay`, `[general]`).


## 1.4.0 — WebRTC video (BUNDLE), immediate-latch audio, DTMF parity, per-Contact REGISTER

This release brings the WebRTC media path to a reliable baseline: audio latches
immediately (no more intermittent one-way or delayed audio), video is negotiated over
a single bundled transport, DTMF reaches full chan_sip parity, and the registrar
implements per-Contact binding semantics to the letter of RFC 3261.

### WebRTC — immediate media latch (audio reliability)

The ICE media tuple is now latched to the peer's source on **every**
integrity-authenticated connectivity check, instead of only on the nominated
(`USE-CANDIDATE`) pair. The previous "latch only the selected pair" behaviour let the
media tuple flap between candidates during the checks and, when a browser stayed in the
controlled role and never nominated, could leave the call deaf — observed as random
10–20 second audio gaps or one-way audio. Latching on any authenticated check follows
the peer's chosen source immediately (RFC 8445 §7.3.1, RFC 7675); SRTP authentication
remains the security gate, and DTLS is (re-)kicked while the handshake is still in
progress. As a permanent **ICE-lite** offerer, GABPBX now completes DTLS and decrypts
SRTP even against a peer that stays controlled and never nominates, so offerer-inbound
calls to a browser connect with two-way audio without a separate role-conflict
workaround.

### WebRTC — video over a single bundled transport (RFC 8843)

GABPBX can now negotiate **audio + video over one bundled ICE/DTLS transport**
(`a=group:BUNDLE`, max-bundle style) as browsers require, demultiplexing the two media
by payload type and RTP MID header extension (RFC 8285) on a single RTP instance. A new
option **`webrtc_video_bundle`** (per-peer and `[general]`, default **off**) enables it —
it takes effect only where `webrtc=yes` is also set; with it off, behaviour is
byte-for-byte unchanged. Video is offered and answered in the initial INVITE in both
directions.

### DTMF — full chan_sip parity

chan_sofia's DTMF handling now matches chan_sip across the board:

- The RTP engine is told when a channel actually uses RFC 2833 telephone-event, so
  `AST_RTP_PROPERTY_DTMF` reflects the negotiated (not merely configured) mode, with a
  distinct effective-mode field tracking runtime vs configured.
- `telephone-event` is advertised in SDP only when the negotiated mode uses RFC 2833
  (`rfc2833` / `auto` / `webrtc`).
- `dtmfmode=auto` is resolved for real at SDP commit, with a **fax-safe** DSP
  reconfigure that never frees a DSP under a live channel (preserving fax/CNG
  detection).
- Inbound SIP `INFO` DTMF parses numeric `Signal=` values (including `10`→`*`,
  `11`→`#`, hook-flash, and Nortel-style bodies).
- `SIPDtmfMode()` re-syncs the runtime mode and the DSP/RTP state, and
  `AST_OPTION_DIGIT_DETECT` is honoured through set/query option.

### Registration — full per-Contact REGISTER (RFC 3261 §10.3)

REGISTER is now processed **per Contact**: each Contact header binds, refreshes, or
de-registers independently, honours its own `;expires` parameter (a per-binding
`expires=0` removes just that binding), and the `200 OK` returns each binding with its
own remaining expiry. A per-Contact de-registration is never rejected by the contact
ACL.

Peer availability now follows Contact expiry as RFC 3261 soft state: `sip show peer
<name> detail` reports an accurate state — **AVAILABLE**, **EXPIRED**, or **IN-CALL** —
instead of a blanket "idle", and a call placed to a peer whose every binding has expired
returns **unavailable** (`CHANUNAVAIL`) rather than being routed to a stale binding.

### TLS — accept TLS 1.2 by default

The Sofia transport defaults to TLS 1.3-only when no minimum version is set, which
rejected the many SIP endpoints that cap at TLS 1.2 (the server aborts the
`ClientHello` with a `protocol_version` alert and registration never completes).
`tls_min_version` now defaults to **1.2** (enabling TLS 1.2 + 1.3); a deployment can
still pin `tls_min_version=1.3`. WebRTC/WSS (TLS 1.3) is unaffected.

### Diagnostics — live decrypted SIP capture (Homer / HEP) + media/fork tracing

SIP over `ws` / `wss` / `tls` is encrypted on the wire, so a packet capture only sees
ciphertext. GABPBX can now tap the Sofia-SIP transport layer and export **every SIP
message, decrypted, in both directions** (`udp` / `tcp` / `tls` / `ws` / `wss`),
including the SDP body — the WebRTC signalling (`a=candidate` / `a=fingerprint` /
`a=setup`). It is off by default (zero overhead) and the capture socket is non-blocking,
so it never stalls the SIP stack.

- **`sip_capture_address`** (`[general]`, e.g. `127.0.0.1:9060`) streams the decrypted
  messages as **HEP** (Homer Encapsulation Protocol) over UDP to a Homer / `sipcapture`
  server — read them with `sngrep -H udp:127.0.0.1:9060`, Homer, or the Wireshark HEP
  dissector. `sip_capture_hep` selects the HEP version (1/2/3, default 3) and
  `sip_capture_id` the capture-agent id.
- **`sip_capture_file`** additionally appends every decrypted message to a plain-text
  file (no Homer needed) — works standalone or alongside HEP.
- Runtime toggles, no reload needed: **`sip set debug capture <host:port>`**
  (`capture off`) and **`sip set debug file <path>`** (`file off`).

Two further default-off tracers aid WebRTC debugging without touching the datapath.
**`rtp set debug ice`** logs the ICE latch/nomination timeline, the `ice_peer` vs
media-source comparison, DTLS record handling, and SRTP first-decrypt. **`sip set debug
fork`** traces the fork lifecycle — child INVITE creation, loser-cancel, winner selection
with the media/fd hand-off, and registrar flow drops — the window into mixed UDP + WSS
forking on a shared address-of-record.


## 1.3.5 — WebRTC inbound (offerer) two-way audio + SIP/SDP diagnostics

### WebRTC — inbound calls to a browser endpoint now connect with two-way audio

Calls **to** a WSS-registered WebRTC peer (where GABPBX is the SDP **offerer**)
previously failed — media never established and the call dropped — while calls
**from** the browser worked. Two root causes are fixed.

#### ICE role-conflict repair — 487 Role Conflict (RFC 8445 §6.1.1 / §7.3.1.1 / §7.2.5.1)
GABPBX is a permanent **ICE-lite** agent, which RFC 8445 requires to take the
**controlled** role. When a browser *answers* GABPBX's offer it also defaults to
**controlled** (offerer = controlling is the usual convention), leaving **both ends
controlled**: neither nominates a candidate pair, no `USE-CANDIDATE` is sent, and
media is never selected — the call is deaf and drops. GABPBX now replies **`487 Role
Conflict`** to an authenticated `ICE-CONTROLLED` binding request, so the full ICE
agent (the browser) flips to controlling, re-checks, and nominates.

#### Defer DTLS until the answer's fingerprint (RFC 5763 / RFC 8842)
As the offerer, GABPBX pre-arms DTLS at offer time. A browser can send its DTLS
`ClientHello` **before** its SIP `200 OK` answer (carrying the `a=fingerprint` trust
anchor) is applied; completing the handshake with no remote fingerprint failed closed
and tore the call down. GABPBX now **defers inbound DTLS records (non-fatally)** until
the fingerprint is installed; DTLS retransmits and the handshake then completes and
verifies. The answerer path is unaffected.

#### Video robustness
A rejected-video answer tears down the pre-armed video DTLS/ICE, and a failed video
media instance can **never hang up the audio call**.

### New diagnostics

#### `sip set debug sdp [on|off]`
A focused SDP/ICE tracer (off by default): dumps the generated **offer SDP**, the
WebRTC **offer-gate** decision, the incoming **answer SDP**, and the offerer
answer-apply path — showing the signaling of **WSS/TLS sessions already decrypted**,
which a packet capture cannot provide.

#### `[from|to|callid]` transaction tag on the SIP debug log
Every `Sofia: …` debug line now carries `[from-user|to-user|callid]` (like GABPBX's
`[channel|uniqueid]`), so log lines are attributable to a specific SIP transaction.


## 1.3.4

A reliability and diagnostics release: connection-oriented bindings now follow the
life of their transport, the `sip show peer` CLI gains a readable summary, and the
RED redundant-text sender is hardened.

### Registration

- **RFC 5626 flow-close.** A connection-oriented binding (WSS / WS / TLS / TCP) is now
  removed promptly when its underlying connection closes, instead of lingering until the
  SIP registration expires — the registrar watches the connection and drops the binding
  on close. This clears stale bindings for WebSocket browser clients that reconnect
  often (for example, a page refresh). A new option `flowclose_emit_unregister`
  (per-peer and `[general]`, default `no`) controls whether the external unregister
  side-effects — AMI `PeerStatus`, BLF / hint device-state, and `regexten` cleanup —
  fire on a flow close. The default keeps it silent (a browser refresh does not flap the
  BLF lamp); set `yes` for an explicit unregister event/state update on each flow close.
  The binding is always removed and routing state always corrected regardless of the
  option.

### CLI

- **`sip show peer <name>` concise summary.** The default view now prints a friendly
  summary — peer name, registration state, endpoint (`user@host:port` via transport),
  context / source, accountcode, contact slots used/allowed, media (codecs, dtmf, nat,
  directmedia), calls (active/limit, ringing, on-hold) and qualify status — followed by
  the full contact list (each with state, TTL, source and user-agent). `sip show peer
  <name> detail` (tab-completed) shows the complete per-field dump: session timers,
  identity headers, network & media, limits & features, fax/T.38, timers & RTP, routing
  & dialplan, security & ACL, registration and contacts.
- **`rtp set debug ice {on|off}`.** Toggleable ICE diagnostics for troubleshooting
  WebRTC media — logs candidate nomination and the post-completion media source.

### Reliability

- **RED (RFC 2198) redundant-text sender hardening.** The per-generation arrays in the
  RTP RED / T.140 redundant-text sender are now bounds-checked, oversized blocks (over
  the RFC 2198 1023-byte length limit) are clamped/rejected, and the redundancy buffer is
  capped — preventing buffer overruns when emitting redundant text.


## 1.3.3 — WebRTC NAT media fixes (chan_sofia)

Three fixes for WebRTC (WSS / SIP.js) clients behind NAT, on top of 1.3.2. All
chan_sofia / RTP, grounded in the relevant RFCs.

### 1. REGISTER contact rebind for WebSocket/NAT clients (no duplicate bindings)
A browser SIP client (SIP.js / Browser Phone) sends a synthetic Contact with no
`;transport` and a fresh random Contact URI + Call-ID on every page reload, so each
reload created a **duplicate** registration binding. chan_sofia now:
- derives the binding transport from the **actual delivering connection** (the Sofia
  tport), not the Contact text — a WebSocket registration is correctly stored as
  `ws`/`wss`, never `udp`;
- rebinds a renewing instance-less device to its existing binding by **source IP (host
  only — the NAT port rotates) + transport + User-Agent**, scoped strictly to the
  registering account, instead of accumulating a new contact;
- prefers RFC 5626 `+sip.instance` / `reg-id` when the client provides them.

Grounded in **RFC 5626** (instance-id/reg-id, AOR binding) and **RFC 7118** (SIP over
WebSocket).

### 2. comedia no longer breaks WebRTC media
For a WebRTC leg, the `comedia` NAT helper used to override the RTP remote with the SIP
signalling source, mis-pointing DTLS/SRTP and breaking audio. **ICE owns the media
destination for WebRTC** (RFC 8445), so comedia is now skipped for WebRTC legs and kept
for plain-RTP NAT phones.

### 3. ICE: media latched to the selected (nominated) pair — fixes 10-20 s audio delay
The ICE responder latched media/DTLS to **every** authenticated connectivity check, so
with a browser probing from several candidates the media tuple flapped and audio took
10-20 seconds to start. Media/DTLS now latches **only to the nominated (USE-CANDIDATE)
pair** and re-latches on each nomination, while still answering every authenticated
check.

Grounded in **RFC 8445** (only the selected pair carries data) and **RFC 7675** (a lite
agent only responds to consent/connectivity checks).


## 1.3.2 — chan_sofia digest-auth & contact-binding fixes for NAT / multi-call phones

Three chan_sofia fixes since 1.3.1, all focused on SIP digest authentication and
contact binding for phones behind NAT (rotating ports, multiple simultaneous calls).

### 1. Digest 401 auth loop fixed - stateless self-validating nonce + per-nonce nc replay cache

**Symptom.** Outbound INVITEs (and BLF/presence SUBSCRIBEs) from registered, multi-call
NAT phones looped on **401 Unauthorized** and never completed; the same accounts worked
under `chan_sip`.

**Root cause.** chan_sofia challenged with `qop="auth"` using a **single per-peer nonce**
plus a **single monotonically-increasing nonce-count (`nc`) shared across all methods**.
A REGISTER (`nc=1`) advanced the shared counter, so a later INVITE/SUBSCRIBE that
legitimately used `nc=1` on the same live nonce looked like a replay -> the server
answered **401 with `stale=true`** -> the client re-authenticated -> looped. (Verified on
production traffic: the second 401 to a correctly-authenticated INVITE carried
`stale=true`.)

**Fix.** A stateless, self-validating nonce **`s1.<timestamp>.<random>.<hmac>`**
(HMAC-SHA256 keyed by a per-process secret), validated by recomputation + a time-to-live,
plus a bounded **per-nonce** `nc` replay cache (inserted only after a valid digest). No
per-peer nonce/nc state remains, so concurrent transactions and different methods no
longer false-stale each other. This matches the canonical stateless-registrar approach.

**RFC.** RFC 2617 §3.2.1 (the `nonce` and `stale` directive), §3.2.2 (`qop`/`nc`/`cnonce`
and the nonce-count replay rule); RFC 7616 §5.4 (Limited-Use Nonce Values), §3.5
(Authentication-Info); RFC 3261 §22.4 (UAS/registrar authentication).

### 2. Force `rport` on the SIP listener - RFC 3581 symmetric response routing

**Symptom.** A NAT'd UA that does **not** add `;rport` to its Via could not register: its
401/200 was routed to the Via sent-by port instead of its NAT-mapped source.

**Fix.** The SIP listener now **always** stamps `rport=<actual source port>` onto every
inbound request's top Via and routes the response symmetrically to the real source, even
when the UA omits `;rport` (sofia `NTATAG_SERVER_RPORT` forced). This also lets chan_sofia
learn the true source address for the registered contact. Matches `chan_sip`'s
`nat=force_rport` default.

**RFC.** RFC 3581 §4 (Server Behavior - the `rport` mechanism / symmetric response routing).

### 3. Contact rebind on REGISTER renewal - no duplicate bindings from NAT port rotation

**Symptom.** A NAT phone changes its source port **and** Contact URI on (almost) every
REGISTER refresh. chan_sofia keyed a binding only on the Contact URI, so each refresh
created a **duplicate** contact; calls then forked to stale (dead) ports and failed.

**Fix.** On an exact-URI miss, chan_sofia now matches the incoming Contact to the same
device's existing binding and **rebinds** it (re-keys the same object) instead of
allocating a duplicate, by key priority:
1. **RFC 5626 `+sip.instance` (+ numeric `reg-id`)** - the stable device identity.
   Distinct `reg-id` values are distinct concurrent flows and are kept separate (not
   collapsed); an instance with no reg-id matches by instance alone; a reg-id with no
   instance is ignored.
2. **REGISTER `Call-ID` + transport** - for instance-less, single-Contact REGISTERs (a
   UA keeps its Call-ID across renewals within a boot cycle).
3. **Conservative NAT fallback** (force_rport peers): source IP (host only, the mapped
   port rotates) + transport + non-empty User-Agent + Contact user.

The same layered match drives the `Expires: 0` de-registration, so a rotated unregister
removes the correct binding. Simultaneous multi-transport (udp/wss/tls) and multi-flow
bindings are preserved (a rebind is a move, so it never grows the contact count or evicts
a sibling flow).

**RFC.** RFC 5626 §4.1 (Instance ID Creation), §11.2 (the `reg-id` Contact header field
parameter), §6 (Registrar Procedures - the binding is AOR + instance-id + reg-id, and a
re-registration for the same combination updates the network-flow information); RFC 3261
§10.2/§10.3 (REGISTER / registrar processing, Call-ID and CSeq ordering); RFC 3327 (the
Path header, preserved across a rebind).

All three fixes are validated on the development server; the contact-rebind change was
additionally exercised under a thread-debug stress (concurrent rotating registrations +
a reload hammer) with no deadlock, lock leak, or duplicate binding.


## 1.3.1 — chan_sofia fixes: correct media profile per transport (406/488) + NAT outbound routing + configurable blacklist ban

Three chan_sofia improvements since 1.3.

### 1. Correct media profile per transport - fixes 406/488 call failures

Calls to ordinary SIP phones could fail with **406 / 488 Not Acceptable** because
chan_sofia offered WebRTC media (DTLS-SRTP) to endpoints that only speak plain RTP.

**The problem.** With `webrtc=yes` enabled (typically once in `[general]` to support
a few WebRTC browser clients), chan_sofia offered the WebRTC secure-media transport
**`m=audio ... UDP/TLS/RTP/SAVPF`** (DTLS-SRTP) on outbound INVITEs to **every**
peer - including plain UDP/TLS desk phones and IP trunks. A plain RTP/AVP endpoint
cannot answer a transport profile it does not support, so per RFC 3264 / RFC 5764 it
rejects the offer with **406/488** and the call fails.

**Root cause.** The outbound offer chose the media profile from the per-peer
`webrtc` flag, which every peer **inherits** from the global `[general] webrtc=yes`,
with no check that the call target was actually a WebRTC endpoint. (`chan_sip` and
`chan_pjsip` both select the RTP profile per endpoint / real capability, never from
a module-wide default.)

**The fix.** `webrtc=yes` (per-peer or inherited) is now only the **enable**; the
actual outbound media profile is **always decided by the target's physical
transport**:

| target | offered media |
|---|---|
| contact registered over **ws/wss** | `UDP/TLS/RTP/SAVPF` (DTLS-SRTP) |
| contact over **udp / tcp / tls** | `RTP/AVP` (or `RTP/SAVP` with `encryption=yes`) |
| **static** peer (`host!=dynamic`) | by its configured `transport=` |

A `udp`/`tls` target is **never** offered DTLS-SRTP, even when `webrtc=yes` is set
explicitly. `webrtc=no` on a peer disables DTLS for it entirely.

**One account, several phones, different technologies - simultaneously.** Because
the decision is **per contact**, a single account can be used from several phones at
once: a WebRTC browser over wss, a desk phone over udp and another over tls,
registered concurrently. A call to that account forks an INVITE to each contact and
offers each the right profile (DTLS-SRTP to the wss browser, plain RTP to the udp/tls
phones) in the same call; inbound calls are answered in kind.

**Compatibility.** No configuration change required. Existing WebRTC browser clients
keep working (auto-detected from their wss registration); plain phones simply stop
receiving the wrong DTLS offer. A realtime backend needs no new column. Inbound
DTLS-SRTP handling is unchanged (offer-driven, RFC 5763).

### 2. Correct outbound routing to a NAT'd peer - the INVITE now reaches the public source

A call placed through a `nat=yes` peer that is registered from behind NAT (a PBX or
phone whose `Contact` carries a private LAN address while it is reached at a learned
public source) could fail: the outbound INVITE was sent to the **private** Contact
address and black-holed, so the call never reached the device and never rang.

**The problem.** For such a peer, chan_sofia correctly learns the public source of
the registration (visible as the per-contact `Source` in `sip show peer`) and uses
it for keepalives and in-dialog requests, **but the initial call INVITE went to the
private Contact host** and was lost. `chan_sip` routes the same peer correctly (it
sends to the registered source).

**Root cause.** The next-hop proxy (`NUTAG_PROXY` = the contact's learned public
source) was computed correctly but passed only at SIP **handle creation**
(`nua_handle`), not on the `nua_invite` that actually sends the request. A
handle-level proxy does not steer the initial INVITE in this SIP stack - only
in-dialog ACK/BYE, which set it per operation, reached the public source. So the
INVITE fell back to its Request-URI, the private Contact.

**The fix.** The learned public source is now applied as `NUTAG_PROXY` at the
**operation level** of every outbound INVITE (initial call, forked-contact INVITEs,
and the directmedia re-INVITE), and is carried onto the winning leg after a fork so
later re-INVITEs keep routing to it. The Request-URI still keeps the (private)
Contact - the packet is simply steered to the public source, matching `chan_sip`
behaviour. The override applies only when a NAT public source is known, so non-NAT
peers and static trunks are unaffected.

### 3. Configurable local blacklist ban window (`blacklist_ban`)

The local anti-abuse blacklist (fail2ban-style: repeated auth / registration /
routing failures from one source IP get the IP blocked at the gate) now has a
configurable ban/decay window via the `[general]` option **`blacklist_ban`**, given
in **MINUTES**:

- `> 0` - ban (and remember the failure counter) for N minutes after the last failure
- `= 0` - banning disabled entirely (no IP is ever blocked or counted)
- default **1440** (24 hours)

Applied live on `sofia reload` (no restart). Inspect/manage with the
`sip show blacklist` / `sip blacklist ...` CLI. Documented in `sofia.conf.sample`.

### Standards

RFC 3264 (offer/answer), RFC 5763 / RFC 5764 (DTLS-SRTP, `UDP/TLS/RTP/SAVPF`),
RFC 4568 (SDES `RTP/SAVP`), RFC 3261 (Contact / next-hop routing), RFC 3581 (rport),
RFC 5626 (managing client-initiated connections through NAT).


## 1.3 — XXH3-64 hashing, all-prime hash tables, core test hash

This release modernises the core string hash that powers every internal hash table
(ao2 containers: peers, dialogs, presence, device state, and more) and retunes
chan_sofia's lookup tables for large, busy deployments.

### What's new

#### XXH3-64 string hashing
`ast_str_hash`, `ast_str_hash_add` and `ast_str_case_hash` now use **XXH3 (64-bit,
xxHash 0.8.3)** instead of the classic byte-wise multiply hash. XXH3 processes the
key in wide words and finishes with a strong avalanche, so it is **substantially
faster on the short keys GabPBX hashes** (peer names, contexts, device names,
mailboxes) while delivering equal-or-better bucket distribution. The implementation
is a small, self-contained, dependency-free header (`include/gabpbx/xxh3_64.h`,
XXH3-64 only) with the hot path fully inlined — no external library, no extra build
flags. The case-insensitive variant folds keys to lowercase in fixed stack windows,
so it never allocates.

#### All-prime hash tables
Every ao2 bucket count (core and chan_sofia) is now a **prime number**, so
`hash % n_buckets` folds in all of the hash's bits and the distribution stays even
regardless of the hash's low-bit behaviour. chan_sofia's lookup tables are sized for
carrier-scale load: peers and presence 65521, publications/dialogs 32749, blacklist
4093, with a dedicated compact index (16381) for the by-source-IP trunk table.

#### `core test hash` — measure it yourself
A new CLI command benchmarks the live build and reports **throughput (Mhash/s),
bandwidth (MB/s) and ns/hash** for both the case-sensitive and case-insensitive
hash, and prints the active algorithm:

    *CLI> core test hash [iterations]

Inspect live table health (buckets, load, longest chain, collisions, chi-squared)
with `core show ao2` (every core container) and `sip show hashstats` (chan_sofia's
own tables).

#### Sharper, safer source-IP trunk identification (chan_sofia)
The by-IP:port fast index that recognises IP trunks now indexes **only static-host
trunks** (never dynamically-registered phones, which are identified by username),
and never stores duplicate keys — keeping source-IP trunk identity unambiguous and
avoiding a registered phone behind a shared NAT being mistaken for a trunk.

### Notes
- Drop-in: no configuration changes required; behaviour is unchanged, only faster.
- xxHash is BSD-2-Clause (Yann Collet); only the XXH3 64-bit one-shot path is vendored.


## 1.2.2 — chan_sofia field fixes

Three field-reported chan_sofia fixes.

**Realtime IP-trunk authentication by source IP+port (chan_sip parity).** Inbound
INVITEs from a realtime `type=peer host=<ip>` trunk are now matched by source IP+port
(with `insecure=port` fallbacks), as chan_sip's `realtime_peer_by_addr` does.
Previously an uncached realtime IP trunk was rejected `401` because the caller-ID in
From did not match the peer name.

**REGISTER nonce-count replay exemption (no more re-registration flap).** Phones that
resend `nc=00000001` on every REGISTER refresh — common across vendors and tolerated by
chan_sip — no longer trip the nonce-count replay guard, which had caused a valid
REGISTER to be answered `401 stale` in a loop. Strict nc-replay protection is kept for
non-idempotent methods (INVITE/MESSAGE).

**Digest authentication uses the configured username (RFC 2617 / RFC 7616).** The
digest A1 hash now uses the peer's configured username (`username=` / `defaultuser=`,
falling back to the section name) instead of the section name, so a peer whose section
name differs from its auth username authenticates correctly. Adds an anti-impersonation
check (the Authorization username must match the configured username) before any hash is
computed or accepted.

Backward-compatible: peers whose section name equals their username are unchanged.


## 1.2.1 — RTP Security Hardening

A focused security-update release hardening the RTP/RTCP media path, T.140 real-time
text, DTLS-SRTP keying, and the WebRTC DataChannel — from a continued multi-round
adversarial security review, every fix anchored to the governing RFC.

### RTP / RTCP
- **RTCP report-block validation** — the SR/RR parser rejects a non-32-bit-aligned
  datagram, bounds the packet by its RFC 3550 §6.4.1 length, and requires the declared
  length to cover the `rc` reception report blocks before reading them — closing an
  out-of-bounds / uninitialised-stack read that leaked via the `RTCPReceived` AMI event.

### T.140 real-time text (RFC 4103 / RFC 2198)
- **Loss-marker overflow** — the missing-text marker is prepended into the frame's
  leading headroom (one per missing T140block, RFC 4103 §5.3) instead of a tail `memmove`
  that wrote past the packet buffer.
- **T140RED parser** — a bounded RFC 2198 F-bit header walk using the full 10-bit block
  length with every offset bounded by the payload size, plus RFC 4103 age-ordered
  redundancy recovery; the RED sender now writes the full 10-bit length too.

### Authentication / transport
- **DTLS-SRTP fingerprint** — the SDP `a=fingerprint` check is enforced unconditionally
  and fails closed when no remote fingerprint is stored (RFC 5763 §5 / RFC 8827 §6.5).
- **DTMF (RFC 4733)** — a telephone-event payload shorter than the 4-octet layout is
  rejected.

### Anti-abuse
- **AMI header injection** — the inbound User-Agent is sanitised before the
  `LockUserAgentReject` event (CWE-93).
- **DataChannel** — the inbound SCTP receive buffer is capped and an oversized
  reassembled message is rejected (CWE-770).

RFC-grounded throughout. Drop-in update over 1.2.

— audited and hardened throughout.


## 1.2 — Security Hardening (chan_sofia)

A focused security-hardening release for the `chan_sofia` SIP channel driver, shaped by
a rigorous, multi-round adversarial security review. Every fix closes a concrete
attack surface and is
anchored to the governing standard — quotes are the load-bearing normative clause,
**verbatim**, with explicit RFC line numbers.

### SIP authentication
- **Digest replay defense** — `qop=auth` nonce-count is validated; a captured
  Authorization header cannot be replayed. RFC 2617 §3.2.2 (lines 657-659): *"if the same
  nc-value is seen twice, then the request is a replay."* RFC 7616 §3.4 (lines 536-539).
- **`uri=` bound to the Request-URI** — RFC 2617 §3.2.2.5 (lines 806-808): *"The
  authenticating server must assure that the resource designated by the 'uri' directive is
  the same as the resource specified in the Request-Line; if they are not, the server
  SHOULD return a 400 Bad Request error."* RFC 7616 §3.4 (lines 711-713).
- **Out-of-dialog MESSAGE authenticated** — RFC 3428 §11 (lines 717-719): *"UAs that
  support the MESSAGE request MUST implement end-to-end authentication, body integrity, and
  body confidentiality mechanisms."*

### WebRTC media security
- **No cleartext media before DTLS** — RFC 8827 §6.5 (lines 832-834): *"Media traffic
  MUST NOT be sent over plain (unencrypted) RTP or RTCP."*
- **Fingerprint trust anchor, fail-closed** — RFC 5763 §5 (lines 436-437): *"The
  integrity of the certificate is ensured through the fingerprint attribute in the SDP."*
- **Reject `a=setup:actpass` in an answer** — RFC 5763 §5 (lines 514-517): *"The answerer
  MUST use either a setup attribute value of setup:active or setup:passive."*

### WebRTC DataChannel (SCTP-over-DTLS)
- **DTLS-role stream parity** — RFC 8832 §6 (lines 322-324): *"If the side is acting as
  the DTLS client, it MUST choose an even stream identifier; if the side is acting as the
  DTLS server, it MUST choose an odd one."*
- **Ordered until acknowledged** — RFC 8832 §6 (lines 354-360): *"before the
  DATA_CHANNEL_ACK message … all other messages containing user data … MUST be sent
  ordered."*
- **SCTP restart handled distinctly** — RFC 6458 §6.1.1 (lines 2426-2437) / RFC 4960
  §5.2.4 (lines 3818-3819).

### SDP offer/answer
- **Answer m-lines match the offer by order; reject with port 0** — RFC 3264 §6 (lines
  468-472, 479-481): *"To reject an offered stream, the port number in the corresponding
  stream in the answer MUST be set to zero."* RFC 8829 §5.3.1 (lines 2402-2406).

### SIP signaling robustness
- **SUBSCRIBE expiry floor (423)** — RFC 6665 §4.2.1.1 (lines 959-964): the notifier *"MAY
  return a 423 (Interval Too Brief) error that contains a 'Min-Expires' header field."*
- **rtcp-mux demux by payload type** — RFC 5761 §4 (lines 217-222).
- **PUBLISH soft state; SIPS over TLS** — RFC 3903 §3 (lines 214-216); RFC 3261 §26.2.2
  (lines 13366-13370) + §19.1.1 (lines 8485-8486): *"For sips:, it is TCP."*
- **`;lr` loose-route detection** — RFC 3261 §19.1.1 (lines 8407-8411).

No new modules — drop-in upgrade. RFC-grounded throughout.


## 1.1.2 — feature-complete chan_sofia + two-way WebRTC video

**`chan_sofia` is now feature-complete** — a drop-in replacement for `chan_sip` with
every `chan_sip` capability and a great deal more — and this release adds **two-way
WebRTC video**.

### Highlights
- **Two-way WebRTC video (VP8/H.264)** — `chan_sofia` both accepts a browser's offered
  video and offers video itself, on a separate non-BUNDLE DTLS/ICE transport,
  complementing two-way WebRTC audio. Two secure-WebSocket browsers see and hear each
  other, with no `pjproject` and no `libnice` in the tree.
- **Opus passthrough** — both-Opus calls relay browser-to-browser without transcoding.
- **Full feature catalogue** — the README and wiki now document every `chan_sofia`
  capability one by one (signaling, media & WebRTC, transports, operations & `chan_sip`
  parity, security & robustness).
- **Engineered for large workloads** — a single Sofia event thread plus a small fixed I/O
  pool, O(1) hash tables sized for carrier load, a bounded register pool under
  registration storms, and a lean high-throughput media engine.
- **Exceptionally robust** — hardened through a rigorous, multi-round adversarial
  correctness, concurrency and security review.

### Fixes in this build
- **Static TLS/TCP/WS/WSS trunk transport** — a peer configured `transport=tls` (e.g. an
  external TLS trunk) now correctly routes its outbound INVITE / OPTIONS / NOTIFY over the
  configured transport; it previously silently defaulted to UDP. Fixed on **both** the
  realtime and the config-file peer paths; a comma-list (`transport=tls,udp`) uses the
  first / preferred token, and `sip show peer` reports the configured transport.

Drop-in migration is two lines in `modules.conf`: `noload => chan_sip.so` / `load =>
chan_sofia.so`.


## 1.1.1 — chan_sofia comes of age

This release turns `chan_sofia` — the Sofia-SIP–based SIP channel driver — into a
broad, production-grade, drop-in replacement for `chan_sip`, and reorganises the
driver into a clean, maintainable module tree. It is a single-line migration for
most deployments:

```ini
noload => chan_sip.so
load   => chan_sofia.so
```

The public `SIP` channel technology, the `sip show …` CLI, the `SIPpeers` /
`SIPshowpeer` AMI surface, the `sippeers` realtime family and the `SIPPEER` /
`SIP_HEADER` dialplan functions are all preserved.

### New SIP capabilities

- **Outbound registration & trunking** — authenticated outbound INVITEs to a
  challenging trunk, fixed outbound REGISTER auth, `sip show registry`,
  `sip unregister <peer>`.
- **RFC 5626 SIP Outbound** on `register =>` trunks (per-peer `sip_outbound`),
  **RFC 3327 Path** on the inbound registrar (`path=yes`), **RFC 3608
  Service-Route** ingestion, and **GRUU** (`+sip.instance` advertisement +
  pub-GRUU used as the dialog Contact).
- **RFC 3262 reliable provisionals / PRACK** (per-peer `rel100`), **RFC 3311
  in-dialog UPDATE**, and **RFC 3326 Q.850 Reason** on BYE/CANCEL
  (`use_q850_reason`).
- **Outbound PUBLISH (RFC 3903)** — publish local dialog/extension state to a
  central presence server, with live reload reconciliation and
  `sip show publications`.
- **Event subscription** — a generic outbound SUBSCRIBE for any event package,
  plus an **outbound MWI SUBSCRIBE watcher** that injects an upstream voicemail
  server's MWI into the local mailbox lamps.
- **Message Waiting Indication** — both solicited (SUBSCRIBE) and **unsolicited**
  (`subscribemwi=no`) message-summary NOTIFY, pushed on REGISTER and on mailbox
  change.
- **Out-of-dialog SIP MESSAGE** (SIMPLE) and **REFER call transfer**.

### Security & transport

- **mutual TLS** — verify client certificates on the inbound TLS listener
  (`tlsverifyclient`), plus **TLS hardening** (`tls_min_version`, `tls_ciphers`,
  `tls_verify_depth`).
- **TCP connection keepalive** (`tcp_keepalive` / `tcp_pingpong`).
- Digest authentication with MD5 **and SHA-256**, anti-downgrade `qop`
  enforcement, contact-ACL preflight on multi-Contact REGISTER, and a
  registration brute-force blacklist.

### Media

- Codec negotiation with a validate-then-commit SDP engine (a rejected re-INVITE
  never disturbs the live call), **SDES-SRTP** staged and applied only after the
  SDP is accepted, **T.38 fax**, direct media, per-peer RTP timeouts, and
  `media_address` to advertise media on a different interface than signalling.
- NAT support — `externip`/`externaddr`/`externhost`/`localnet`, `comedia`,
  `force_rport`, and registered-transport-aware routing for TCP/TLS phones.

### Diagnostics & operations

- **Per-call SIP history** with a verbose call-analysis pass (outcome, failure
  reason, timeline, negotiated codecs) and a source/destination capture filter —
  including a retained ring so a call stays inspectable after hangup.
- New CLI: `sip qualify peer`, `sip notify`, `sip show channel`,
  `sip show channelstats`, `sip show history`, `sip history clear`.
- Presence / BLF (dialog-info, PIDF) with device-state on call transitions, and
  the full realtime peer/registration model.

### Engineering

- `chan_sofia.c` has been **split into a `channels/sofia/` module tree**
  (AMI, CLI, SDP, presence, T.38, PUBLISH, MESSAGE, transfer, GRUU, subscribe,
  history, …) for maintainability.
- Extensive **robustness work**: a canonical channel→pvt→peer lock order,
  teardown-race and reload use-after-free fixes, bounded SDP build buffers, and
  the snapshot-under-lock discipline throughout — validated under a concurrency
  stress harness with `DEBUG_THREADS`.

Built and tested against GCC 12 (Debian 12). Existing Asterisk copyright
notices and GPLv2 terms are preserved in the source files where they apply.


## 1.1

`chan_sofia` matures into a more capable SIP driver, building on the 1.0 correctness
base: a modular source layout, new SIP capabilities, and continued performance,
forwarding and reliability work.

### Modular source layout

- **The driver is now a core plus cohesive subsystem modules.** `channels/chan_sofia.c`
  keeps the channel technology vtable, event dispatch, authentication, and the call /
  register / configuration core; the subsystems that form clean functional boundaries
  move into `channels/sofia/`: `sofia_sdp.c` (SDP offer/answer), `sofia_t38.c` (T.38
  fax), `sofia_presence.c` (presence/BLF), `sofia_publish.c` (outbound PUBLISH),
  `sofia_ami.c` (AMI actions), `sofia_cli.c` (CLI commands) and `sofia_blacklist.c`,
  alongside the existing `sdp_crypto.c` / `srtp.c`. It is still one loadable module
  (`chan_sofia.so`), the core is ~44% smaller, and the change is **behavior-identical** —
  every extraction was wire-validated and lock-checked.

### New SIP capabilities

- **Presence / BLF.** Inbound `SUBSCRIBE` → `NOTIFY` for `dialog-info+xml`, `pidf+xml`
  and `xpidf+xml`, so busy-lamp-field and presence watchers light up. Governed by the
  existing `allowsubscribe` / `subscribecontext` gates.
- **Outbound PUBLISH (RFC 3903).** Publishes local extension/dialog state to a central
  state server (`publish_server`, with reload reconciliation and `sip show publications`).
- **Outbound REGISTER authentication.** chan_sofia now answers a `401`/`407` challenge
  when registering to an upstream trunk.
- **Connection keepalive.** `tcp_keepalive` / `tcp_pingpong` keep TCP/TLS connections to
  carriers and NAT'd endpoints alive.
- **Instance advertisement on outbound REGISTER** (`gruu`) — adds `+sip.instance` to the
  outbound Contact.
- **TLS hardening** (`tlsverify`, default off) — peer-certificate verification policy for
  TLS transports.
- **Digest algorithm offer** (`auth_algorithms = both | md5 | sha256`) — controls which
  algorithm the `WWW-Authenticate` challenge offers, with anti-downgrade enforcement.
- **CLI additions:** `sip show registry`, `sip unregister`, `sip show publications`, plus
  peer-name tab completion on `sip show peer`.

### Performance & scaling

- **Bounded REGISTER offload pool** (`register_pool`, default **OFF**). Offloads the
  realtime-database writes for REGISTER to a small fixed pool of AOR-keyed worker
  lanes, so a slow-database REGISTER storm no longer head-of-line-blocks
  INVITE/OPTIONS on the single signaling thread. Bounded (fixed lanes + in-flight
  cap), reversible at runtime with `sofia reload`, and byte-for-byte the legacy
  inline path until enabled. Same separation Kamailio gets from its async
  usrloc/worker model and FreeSWITCH from its thread pools.
- **Carrier-scale hash sizing.** Peer and dialog hash bucket caps enlarged
  (`MAX_PEER_BUCKETS` 16381 → 65521, `MAX_DIALOG_BUCKETS` 8191 → 32749) so O(1)
  peer/dialog lookups keep a load factor below 1 into tens of thousands of peers
  and high concurrent-dialog volume.

### Call forwarding

- **`forceddiversion`** (per-peer). Forces a trunk-owned DID as the diverting party
  in the outbound `Diversion` header (RFC 5806) on forwarded calls, so a carrier can
  validate the diversion against a number it provisions for the trunk. Emitted only
  when the channel carries a redirect marker; empty (default) keeps the legacy
  data-driven behavior.

### Reliability

- **Continued concurrency and memory-safety hardening** across the registration, media,
  presence and teardown paths, extending the 1.0 correctness work. A re-INVITE that is
  rejected now leaves the live call fully unchanged (validate-then-commit SDP handling);
  REGISTER side effects run outside the peer lock; and the SUBSCRIBE/NOTIFY, fork and
  qualify paths were swept for use-after-free and lock-order issues — each fix verified
  under a thread-debug build with `core show locks`.


## 1.0

The first tagged GABPBX release. `chan_sofia` — the Sofia-SIP based `chan_sip`
replacement — reaches a stable, production-ready baseline.

### Why this release matters

`chan_sofia` lets you move off the aging `chan_sip` without rewriting your
deployment. It keeps the public compatibility surface that existing systems
depend on:

- Channel technology `SIP` (so `Dial(SIP/peer)` is unchanged).
- CLI: `sip show peers`, `sip show peer`, `sip show channels`, `sip set debug`.
- AMI: `SIPpeers`, `SIPshowpeer`, `SIPqualifypeer`, `SIPshowregistry`, `SIPnotify`.
- Realtime family `sippeers`.
- Dialplan functions `SIPPEER`, `SIPCHANINFO`, `SIP_HEADER`, `CHECKSIPDOMAIN`.

For most systems the switch is a single change in `modules.conf`:

```ini
noload => chan_sip.so
load   => chan_sofia.so
```

### What chan_sofia gives you

Registration, digest authentication (MD5 and SHA-256), inbound and outbound
calls, re-INVITE / hold, attended and blind transfer, multi-contact forking,
RTP with NAT handling, SRTP, session timers, MWI, T.38 fax, UDP / TCP / TLS
transports, per-peer permit/deny ACLs and call limits, and PostgreSQL realtime
peers — all on the maintained Sofia-SIP NUA stack.

### The 1.0 focus: concurrency and memory safety

1.0 is a deep correctness pass that makes the driver dependable under real
traffic and routine operations:

- **One authoritative locking model.** A single block at the top of
  `channels/chan_sofia.c` defines the rule the whole driver follows: one SIP
  event-loop thread owns the mutable peer and dialog state; every other thread
  (dialplan, CLI, AMI, bridge, scheduler, registration and qualify) reads it
  under the `channel -> pvt -> peer` lock order. Every inline lock note in the
  file is an instance of that model.
- **Dialog teardown races closed.** Every in-dialog request and response that
  touches a dialog is revalidated and reference-counted for the life of its
  handler, so a concurrent hangup can no longer free the dialog underneath it.
- **`sip reload` is safe under load.** Peer string fields, and the global
  localnet and contact ACL lists, are read under locks, so a reload that frees
  and rebuilds them is never observed half-built by a thread that is setting up
  or running a call.
- **Lock-order and lifetime fixes.** The T.38 reINVITE-timeout path no longer
  inverts the channel and dialog locks; transfer, fork-winner and
  bridged-channel paths no longer leak a reference or a SIP handle; AMI and
  hangup paths snapshot owner-derived data under the dialog lock.
- **Exercised, not just reasoned about.** A repeatable stress procedure — a SIP
  call flood plus a `sip reload` loop, run together under a thread-debug
  (`DEBUG_THREADS`) build so locks can be inspected with `core show locks` —
  drives these paths. The 1.0 set passed with no crash, deadlock or memory
  error.

See the [chan_sofia wiki page](https://github.com/garacil/gabpbx/wiki/Chan-Sofia)
for the full concurrency model, configuration reference and operational recipes.
